45417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087838 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429672 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429673 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Intercepts and Intersections of Hyperbolas" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1033 874 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1034 0.7689827632703577 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051028 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7b7dc93e-4cab-570c-b330-4cc489161751" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186049477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186087729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429653 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1029 #edb/ref 17592186429654 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1031 "Finding Intercepts of Ellipses" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1033 850 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1034 1.2655324105615433 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186051081 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "7fa30ad3-9041-5912-9f1e-90bd24bebbe8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068460 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068460 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the $y$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-1, 4)$ and has a slope of $2$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068460 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ad21d3a-bf40-5b65-8ba6-320d853e6cff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068460 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the line passes through the point $(-1,4)$ and its slope is $2$, we substitute $x=-1$, $y=4$, and $m=2$ into the equation in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} y &= mx + b \\\\ (4) &= (2)(-1) + b \\\\ 4 &= -2 + b \\\\ 6 &= b \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is $b=6$. The coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,6)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068461 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12870" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068461 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the equation of the line that passes through the points $(-2,4)$ and $(10,10)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fdc3a6e9-080b-5e2a-b5a4-3af01c7018ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068461 #edb/ref 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b$.\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\nm = \\frac{y_{2} - y_{1}}{x_{2} - x_{1}} = (10 - 4)/(10 - (-2)) = \\frac{6}{12} = \\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope\n\n$$\nm=\\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\ninto the slope-intercept formula\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nwe reach\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac 1 2 x + b\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b$. We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(10,10)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= \\frac{1}{2}x + b \\\\\n10 &= \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 10 + b \\\\\n10 &= 5 + b \\\\\nb &= 10 - 5 \\\\\nb &= 5\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=\\dfrac 1 2 x + 5\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068462 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12871" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068462 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the $x$-intercept of the line that passes through the point $(-3,2)$ and has a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{2}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068462 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ec5bebf6-58d8-5b4d-900c-3c024628647c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068462 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we need to find the equation of the line. We know that\n\n$$\nm=-\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nso we can write the equation of the line as\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x + b\n$$\n\nIt remains to find the $y$-intercept of the line. We can do that by substituting the point $(-3,2)$ into the equation and solving for $b$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + b \\\\\n(2) &= -\\frac{1}{2}(-3) + b \\\\\n2 &= \\frac{3}{2} + b \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2} &= b\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the line is\n\n$$\ny=-\\dfrac{1}{2}x+\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0$. So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n0 &= -\\frac{1}{2}x + \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{1}{2}x &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nx &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$-intercept is $x=1$. The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(1,0)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068463 #edb/ref 1069 "e-12872" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068463 #edb/ref 1071 "A vertical line $l$ passes through the point $(-6,3)$. Calculate the coordinates of the $x$-intercept of $l$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068463 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "409dfb1f-7400-5539-9dea-6c2d1b6b7ef0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068463 #edb/ref 1074 "On any vertical line, all points have the same $x$-coordinate.\n\nThe $x$-coordinate of the point $(-6,3)$ is $-6$, so the $x$-coordinate of the $x$-intercept is also $-6$.\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(-6,0)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049716 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Y-Intercept of a Line Given a Point on the Line and Its Slope" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068460 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068924 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "bc85ca17-8a08-5473-9473-dce954132bd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069510 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235491 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Equation of a Line That Passes Through Two Points" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068461 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068925 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "5e985ae8-05ba-590a-b504-4c84884679f4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069044 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186235493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the X-Intercept of a Line Given a Point and a Slope" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068926 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "63461668-4f76-52a4-8115-1ab135eef9e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069245 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069454 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1039 "Finding the Intercept of a Horizontal or Vertical Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068927 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "f66989ce-aac5-5737-b60b-6ed703634cd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1045 "Properties of Lines Given in Slope-Intercept Form" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069912 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068943 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "f525c0ff-8172-5646-b7ba-2dd13dd2cdf5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069912 #edb/ref 1061 67150 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "32960d35-0132-5c05-a0dc-e73600e6a188" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069912 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070012 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069913 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069913 #edb/ref 1061 67151 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069913 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "05d03a89-9aa4-5b0f-97cd-69809a74c97d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069913 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069914 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069914 #edb/ref 1061 67155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069914 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fab7c6fb-b6fe-5b55-adee-114f45b728d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069914 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068925 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069916 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1061 67159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "27dfa118-efd1-5317-966f-90a083509c55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069915 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186070013 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069917 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1061 67160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ebe74c08-cc12-564f-b8bf-ce093355d7a9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069916 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1061 67164 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8c918cb7-6f6b-5686-99e8-54b94b6009e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069917 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068927 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1064 "The **$y$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $y$-axis. For instance, consider the line that passes through the point $(1,-1)$ and has a slope of $3$. How can we find the $y$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/c3/c307a60266b77cb497a5c5b02a6d9518a3b31e22b1ec97afb1d2b40f488982cb.png)\n\nWe already know values for $x$, $y$ and $m$, so we can just substitute them into the equation\n\n$$\ny=mx+b\n$$\n\nand then solve for $b$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= mx + b \\\\\n(-1) &= (3)(1) + b \\\\\n- 1 &= 3 + b \\\\\nb &= -4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $y$-intercept is\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}b}=-4\n$$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,-4)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1065 8059 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f9c0e73-8a80-5472-bf82-4c78c873c181" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070012 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1064 "The **$x$-intercept** of a line is where the line intersects the $x$-axis. For instance, consider the line $y = 2x - 4$. How can we find the $x$-intercept of this line?\n\n![](images/57/57a31eab932c948358e7ab6e428707a6a4b9f551bc956554c3a16b659b5f7988.png)\n\nAt the $x$-intercept, the $y$-coordinate is equal to $0$. So, we can substitute $y=0$ into the equation and then solve for $x$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 2x - 4 \\\\\n(0) &= 2x - 4 \\\\\n4 &= 2x \\\\\n2 &= x \\\\\nx &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $x$**-intercept** is $x=2$. The coordinates of the $x$-intercept are $(2,0)$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1065 8060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fbd0b44d-8460-5058-9b94-c21852d09a4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186070013 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding the X-Intercept of a Straight Line" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
